#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065058} :activity/id #uuid "36b1e329-01bb-56a1-ac8d-381acb37472d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065428 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate an integral like\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{2x+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x} \\, \\textrm dx = \\ln |x|,$ but our case is different because now it's a whole function $(2x+1)$ that is in the denominator.\n\nFor integrals like the one above, we can use a substitution. First, let $u=2x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\,  \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and solve it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we write the final answer in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|2x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|2x+1|+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{ln}|2x+1|]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(2x+1)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "539b2d2a-d59d-59a2-8b1b-cd7fbe582c04" :tutorial/math-academy-id 830 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "61778bbc-1b89-5ca5-ac1e-60c27caacd90" :step/math-academy-id 1598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065059}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064206 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064077 :question/id #uuid "d6501a20-6ebd-5786-870e-3527423b6332" :question/math-academy-id "e-449" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{5x+2}\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=5x+2.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{5x+2}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{5x+2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc4294d6-3d60-5c9e-a862-863e1d129049" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480729} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332721}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function"} :step/id #uuid "13c863f7-4fc0-517f-b678-f6be527e6273" :step/math-academy-id 1599 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065060}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064207 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064078 :question/id #uuid "115b3fb1-1041-532a-b5fa-4d8a53c9ab2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-450" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x},$ writing your final answer as a single function." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=1+2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+2x}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C = \\dfrac 1 2\\ln K$ for another arbitrary constant $K > 0,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K\\cdot |1+2x|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1+2x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x  = \\dfrac 1 2 \\ln \\left(K |1+2x|\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491407} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596710}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"} :step/id #uuid "41e4fa5d-0cf2-527d-a1cf-4680f7fe8501" :step/math-academy-id 1602 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065429 :tutorial/content "In general,\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{ax+b}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 a \\ln|ax+b| + C.\n$$\n\nThese types of integrals come up quite often in calculus, so it's worth remembering this formula." :tutorial/id #uuid "933683cf-f7fa-50f2-9f11-4522ca637c77" :tutorial/math-academy-id 833 :tutorial/title "The General Formula"} :step/id #uuid "61894f3b-5eea-5454-9b95-195f28437d17" :step/math-academy-id 1605 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064208 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064079 :question/id #uuid "2c4912bd-290a-5074-b11b-459c77b7e238" :question/math-academy-id "e-451" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\left(\\dfrac{1}{1+2x} + \\dfrac{1}{1-2x}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{1+2x}+\\frac{1}{1-2x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1+2x}{1-2x}|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+2x}{1-2x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481494} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339120}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"} :step/id #uuid "57491cdf-9108-58c2-927a-492922f20e8e" :step/math-academy-id 1606}] :activity/title "Integrating Linear Rational Functions Using Substitution" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064422}]}
